ening"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":4,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":4,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":4,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"log-reader":1,"material":3}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":47,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Lock released, rig state untouched at 조립, STATUS.md at 110 lines. Cycle 34 is closed.\n\n## What this cycle did\n\n**N1 is accepted and D1 is unblocked** — the deliverable-level result. The VI-level fixture comparison had never actually run; cycle 33 wrote `n1_gpuk_vi_fixture.py` and died before executing it, leaving only the DLL-level half. Run properly (7/7 gates, rc=0, 10,043 frames × 5 beads), `GPU_kernel_v1.vi` reproduces the DLL numbers exactly — the LabVIEW wrapper is numerically transparent — and on the 10,018 frames before the first bead loss it meets the user's criterion with zero exceedances on all three axes (max |dx| 4.86e-07, |dy| 4.68e-07 against 1e-6; |dz| 1.28e-05 against ~1e-4). Every violation in the whole-fixture figure is bead 4 after bead 4's own loss, while the other four beads are clean at identical frame indices — a loss artefact, not a late divergence. Two things are flagged to the user rather than buried: that the acceptance is evaluated on the pre-loss window, and one bead-frame in 50,215 where the two kernels pick adjacent z-lookup indices.\n\n**OPEN 55 closed without touching the motor-limit parser.** The peer's refutation was tested rather than believed: a send-mode transcript returns `None`, confirming the peer's own criterion — but the call-site census shows every production path into `tmx_from` is limits-set only, so the regression is unreachable and its failure direction refuses motion. I decided the remaining design question too, rather than passing it on: delete rule 4 entirely instead of making the parser mode-aware, because the `LIMITS` line prints *after* the write and can only ever echo what we sent. That patch is pre-decided for the next cycle.\n\n## The thing worth reading\n\nThe retrospective's finding is that a `claude -p` session **cannot** collect background results after ending its turn. Cycle 33 dispatched two opus/max peers, ended its turn, and the harness killed both at the 600 s ceiling — two paid reviews dead with no cost line, and no retrospective for that cycle at all. This session paid $5.88 re-asking. It is the third occurrence of a class already written into `CLAUDE.md:316-318`.\n\nI only escaped it by holding the turn open with a foreground wait loop while the retrospective ran; ending the turn would have killed the very review that found the fault. That is now in STATUS OPEN 54 as a rule, and the orphan detector the peer asks for is recorded in `docs/violation-decisions.md` as deliberately **not built** — Pre-decided 2 is the user's standing no-more-devices order, and it is first in line if they lift it. I also corrected one detail the peer got wrong: the duplicate dispatch was visible to me in the brief, but it was never written into any document, and a brief is not a record.\n\nAlso resolved: all four `doc_ingest` contradictions, including an arithmetic error in the plan's own click-deviation figure and two items both numbered `Pre-decided 9`, which made \"cite the number\" ambiguous in a file whose instruction is to cite the number.","ttft_ms":2182,"type":"result","duration_ms":2517720,"uuid":"8be7f0b4-26dc-4ee4-bab1-5f43307fc66f","ttft_stream_ms":1805,"time_to_request_ms":267,"queued_turn_count":0}
